\DOC X86_ACCSIM_TAC

\TYPE {X86_ACCSIM_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> int list -> int list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes a straight-line block of x86 machine code in an {ensures}
goal, accumulating arithmetic/flag facts as new assumptions and reducing the
goal to the remaining postcondition obligations, or closing it outright.

\DESCRIBE
{X86_ACCSIM_TAC execth anums snums} takes {execth : thm * (thm option array)},
the precomputed execution data for the code being simulated (its length theorem
together with per-instruction decoding information); {anums : int list}, the
step numbers whose results are recorded as "accumulated" arithmetic equations;
and {snums : int list}, the range of instruction steps to simulate. Applied to
a goal {A ?- ensures ...}, it either closes the goal outright or leaves one
subgoal whose conclusion is the residual arithmetic/word content of the
postcondition, re-expressed in terms of freshly introduced symbolic variables
for the intermediate results and for the final machine state. In the latter
case:
{
      A ?- t
   ================================ X86_ACCSIM_TAC execth anums snums
      A, h1 ... hn ?- u
}
\noindent where {h1 ... hn} are real-arithmetic equations for the accumulated
steps (relating products, sums and carry bits of the introduced variables), the
rewritten precondition facts transported to the final state, equations giving
the final state's register, flag and memory reads, the loaded-code fact, and a
{MAYCHANGE} relation between the initial and final symbolic states.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, on {ensures} goals for x86
code with a straight-line instruction range.

\ENDDOC
